#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
baja-server: the fundraising donation endpoints, installed ON the server.

    sudo python3 install-donation-endpoint.py /opt/baja-server && sudo systemctl restart baja-server
    curl -s localhost:8080/stripe/donation-session

TWO KEYS. Donations read STRIPE_DONATION_SECRET_KEY and the software subscription keeps
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY, because they are different Stripe accounts and a price, a customer and a payment
each belong to one account. If the donation key is not set, donations fall back to the subscription
key, so installing this changes no behaviour until the second key exists. set-stripe-key.sh puts
either key in place.

WHY THIS EXISTS. deploy/deploy.sh builds TypeScript on a laptop and rsyncs the result over ssh; run
from the server itself it tries to ssh into the machine it is already on, and wants a key that is not
there. This installs the same code without a build: the COMPILED form (tsc ES2020, the project's own
settings, so async/await stays native and no helper is needed) goes into dist/index.js, and
src/index.ts is patched too when that file is present, so a later rebuild on this box does not drop it.

    GET  /stripe/donation-session   says what the route is, whether a donation key is set, and
        whether donations are on their own Stripe account or still sharing the subscription's
    POST /stripe/donation-session   { amount, currency, campaign, path, returnUrl } -> { url }
        A one-off Stripe Checkout session for the amount a donor chose on the public display. It names
        no payment_method_types, so Stripe offers every method enabled on the account that is eligible
        for the amount, currency and country: card, Apple Pay, Google Pay, Link, Cash App, bank debits.

Both of the two below are restricted to the accounts in FUNDRAISE_ADMINS (comma-separated, default
jeffmilto@gmail.com), by middleware registered above them: they carry the money, the fees and the
donors' names and email addresses, where the display and the sync carry only what a campaign already
publishes. This server verifies no tokens -- a request says who it is and is believed -- so that is
account gating of the same kind as the rest of the platform, not authentication.

    GET  /f/<code>                  the short link a campaign is shared by. It answers with the
        campaign's own title, description and picture as link-preview tags, then sends a browser on
        to the display. The display's own URL cannot do this: nginx hands /app/... to the Angular
        shell, and Facebook, iMessage, WhatsApp and Slack read the HTML without running the script
        that draws the campaign, so every campaign shared from there showed the shell's own card.
    GET  /f/<code>/i                that card's picture: the campaign's own, or an awareness ribbon.

    GET  /stripe/payment-methods    what the Stripe account has turned on, so the console can answer
        "will donors be offered Apple Pay?" from the account rather than from a guess. Nothing in this
        code decides that list: the session names no payment_method_types, so Stripe offers whatever
        is enabled and whatever the donor's device and country support.

    POST /fundraise/campaigns       { user } -> every .fund campaign in that user's public folder,
        with its totals, for the administration page. It happens here because the folder name under
        the user-data root is an AES encoding of the address, which the browser cannot work out.

    POST /stripe/donation-report    { path } -> per-donation gross, Stripe's fee and the net, plus
        the totals. The display's number is GROSS; the bank gets the net. Payment intents are
        searched by the campaign path in their metadata, so a busy account cannot hide one
        campaign's donations behind another's.

    POST /stripe/donation-sync      { path } -> { added, amount, raised, donors }
        Folds sessions Stripe reports as PAID for that campaign into the campaign document, once each
        (the session id is recorded with the gift). This is what makes a donation show up on the
        display: a paid session is otherwise known to Stripe and to nobody else. One Stripe lookup per
        campaign per 15 seconds, however many screens are watching. Donor names are written only if
        the campaign sets donate.showNames, because the document is public; the email never is.

The pieces are installed independently, so a server that already has some of them gets only what
is missing. Each goes in beside the Stripe billing-portal route and needs the `app`, `stripeClient`,
`stripeReady`, `userData`, `fs` and `path` that file already has. If it cannot find them, or cannot
find a place to insert, it refuses and changes nothing.

Every file it edits is backed up beside itself (.before-donation), the patched dist is checked with
`node --check` before it replaces anything, and nothing is written unless every piece applies. Safe to
run twice. The sourcemap comment at the end of dist/index.js is now off by the inserted lines, which
affects stack-trace line numbers and nothing else; the next real deploy restores it.
"""
